examples.question_text:
Name the pair of perpendicular lines. Write a perpendicular statement.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (-2,-1) node[left] {$A$} -- (2,1) node[right] {$B$};
\draw (-1,2) node[above] {$C$} -- (1,-2) node[below] {$D$};
\draw (-0.35,-2) node[below] {$E$} -- (0.35,2) node[above] {$F$};
\draw (0.30,0.15) -- (0.15,0.45) -- (-0.15,0.30);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Lines $p$ and $q$ meet at the marked angle. Are they perpendicular?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (-2,0) -- (2,0) node[right] {$p$};
\draw (268:1.8) -- (88:1.8) node[above] {$q$};
\draw (0.55,0) arc[start angle=0,end angle=88,radius=0.55];
\node at (44:0.95) {$88^{\circ}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The diagram shows rectangle $PQRS$.

a. Are opposite sides $PQ$ and $RS$ perpendicular?

b. Name one pair of perpendicular sides.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (0,0) node[below left] {$P$} -- (3.6,0) node[below right] {$Q$} -- (3.6,2.2) node[above right] {$R$} -- (0,2.2) node[above left] {$S$} -- cycle;
\draw (0.28,0) -- (0.28,0.28) -- (0,0.28);
\draw (3.32,0) -- (3.32,0.28) -- (3.6,0.28);
\draw (3.32,2.2) -- (3.32,1.92) -- (3.6,1.92);
\draw (0.28,2.2) -- (0.28,1.92) -- (0,1.92);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The diagram is drawn on a square grid. Is $KL \perp MN$ true or false? Consider the full lines if the segments are extended.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{gridcolour}{HTML}{CCCCCC}
\draw[step=0.5,draw=gridcolour,thin] (-2.5,-2) grid (2.5,2);
\draw[thick] (-1.5,-1.5) node[below left] {$K$} -- (-1.5,1.5) node[above left] {$L$};
\draw[thick] (0,-0.5) node[below] {$M$} -- (2,-0.5) node[below] {$N$};
\draw[thick] (-0.5,-1.5) node[below] {$P$} -- (2,1) node[above] {$Q$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
True. $KL$ follows a vertical grid line and $MN$ follows a horizontal grid line. Their full lines meet at $90^{\circ}$.

$KL \perp MN$

The dashed extension and square below show their right-angle meeting point.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{gridcolour}{HTML}{CCCCCC}
\draw[step=0.5,draw=gridcolour,thin] (-2.5,-2) grid (2.5,2);
\draw[thick] (-1.5,-1.5) node[below left] {$K$} -- (-1.5,1.5) node[above left] {$L$};
\draw[thick] (0,-0.5) node[below] {$M$} -- (2,-0.5) node[below] {$N$};
\draw[thick] (-0.5,-1.5) node[below] {$P$} -- (2,1) node[above] {$Q$};
\draw[dashed] (-1.5,-0.5) -- (0,-0.5);
\draw (-1.22,-0.5) -- (-1.22,-0.22) -- (-1.5,-0.22);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

